('h2', "1.1  Introduction"),
('p', "Health care is not merely a technical occupation; it is a **moral enterprise**. A Medical Laboratory Technologist handles a patient's blood, tissue, body fluids and \u2014 most importantly \u2014 a patient's **secrets, fears and future**. A single wrongly reported HIV test, a mislabelled blood group, or one careless remark in a corridor can destroy a life. Technical competence alone therefore cannot make a good laboratory professional; competence must be married to **character**. The study of that character, of the rules that govern it, and of the duties that flow from it, is the subject of *professionalism and ethics*."),
('p', "The word **ethics** comes from the Greek *ethos*, meaning character, custom or habitual conduct. The word **moral** comes from the Latin *mores*, meaning customs or manners. **Profession** comes from the Latin *professio* \u2014 'to declare publicly', because historically a professional publicly *professed* an oath to serve society before being allowed to practise. This origin is important: from the very beginning, a profession was defined not by what it earned but by what it **promised**."),

('def', ("Ethics",
         "Ethics is the branch of philosophy (moral philosophy) that deals with the systematic study of what is **right and wrong, good and bad, just and unjust** in human conduct, and provides the standards or principles by which human actions and character are judged.",
         ["**Medical Ethics:** the branch of applied ethics that studies moral values, principles and rules governing the conduct of persons engaged in the practice of medicine and allied health sciences, and their relationship with patients, colleagues, society and the State.",
          "**Morals:** an individual's personal beliefs about right and wrong, usually derived from family, religion, culture and upbringing.",
          "**Values:** deeply held convictions about what is desirable and important, which act as the foundation of both morals and ethics."])),

('def', ("Profession",
         "A profession is an occupation that requires **prolonged specialised education and training**, is based on a **body of scientific knowledge**, involves **intellectual skill and judgement**, functions with a considerable degree of **autonomy**, is regulated by its own **code of ethics** and licensing body, and is practised primarily as a **service to society** rather than purely for personal gain.")),

('def', ("Professionalism",
         "Professionalism is the **habitual and judicious demonstration** of the knowledge, technical skills, communication, ethical values, attitudes, behaviour and accountability that are expected of a member of a profession in the service of the individual patient and the community.",
         ["In one exam-friendly line: *Professionalism = Competence + Character + Commitment + Accountability.*"])),

('quote', ("Ethics is knowing the difference between what you have a right to do and what is right to do.", "Potter Stewart")),

('h2', "1.2  Why Study Ethics? (Need and Importance)"),
('n', [
    "**To protect the patient.** The patient is in a vulnerable, dependent position and cannot judge technical quality; ethics is the patient's only real protection.",
    "**To guide decision-making in grey areas.** Textbooks tell you *how* to do a test; ethics tells you *whether* you should, and what to do with the result.",
    "**To maintain public trust in the health system.** Health care runs on trust; a single scandal (fake reports, organ racket, sex determination) damages the credibility of an entire hospital or profession.",
    "**To provide a uniform standard of conduct.** A code of ethics ensures that a patient receives the same minimum standard of respect and care from every technologist, everywhere.",
    "**To protect the professional.** A technologist who follows ethical protocol (consent, documentation, confidentiality, reporting through proper channels) is legally far safer.",
    "**To resolve conflicts of interest and conflicts of duty.** For example, loyalty to an employer versus honesty about a faulty analyser.",
    "**To act as a self-regulating mechanism.** A profession that disciplines itself avoids harsher regulation being imposed from outside.",
    "**To humanise technology.** As laboratories become automated, the risk of treating a patient as a barcode increases; ethics keeps the person visible behind the sample.",
    "**Legal necessity.** Many ethical duties (consent, confidentiality, non-abandonment, record keeping) are now enforceable in law; ignorance is not a defence.",
    "**Professional satisfaction.** Ethical practice reduces moral distress and burnout and increases pride in one's work."
]),

('h2', "1.3  Characteristics (Hallmarks) of a Profession"),
('p', "Sociologists list the following features which distinguish a **profession** from an ordinary **job** or **occupation**. Medical Laboratory Technology satisfies all of them, which is why an MLT is a professional and not merely a technician."),
('n', [
    "**A systematic body of theoretical knowledge** \u2014 haematology, microbiology, biochemistry, pathology, immunology.",
    "**Prolonged, specialised, formal education** in a recognised institution, ending in a degree or diploma.",
    "**Professional authority and expertise** \u2014 the professional, not the client, decides the technically correct course of action.",
    "**Autonomy of practice** \u2014 the freedom and responsibility to exercise independent judgement (for example, rejecting a haemolysed sample).",
    "**A code of ethics** which is binding on all members.",
    "**Registration / licensure** by a statutory council, and the power of that council to remove a name from the register.",
    "**A professional association or organisation** that sets standards and speaks for the members.",
    "**Service orientation / altruism** \u2014 the interest of the patient is placed above self-interest.",
    "**Accountability and responsibility** \u2014 answerable to the patient, employer, council, courts and society.",
    "**Continuing professional development** \u2014 an obligation to remain up to date lifelong.",
    "**Public recognition and social status**, with a corresponding expectation of higher standards of behaviour.",
    "**Distinct professional culture** \u2014 shared language, norms, symbols and dress."
]),

